Abstract

This dissertation develops a rigorous theoretical framework in which the classical spacetime metric emerges as the pullback of the Quantum Fisher Information Metric (QFIM) of an underlying family of quantum states. No additional quantum‑gravity structures are assumed. The framework is minimal and is built from three established pillars: holographic duality, entanglement thermodynamics, and the Fisher‑information encoding of gravitational actions.

The thesis makes three central contributions, each supported by complete proofs or existence theorems.

First, we present a fully rigorous derivation of Jackiw‑Teitelboim gravity from the Sachdev‑Ye‑Kitaev model. By recognising that the true dynamical degrees of freedom are boundary time reparametrisations, we construct the correct infinite‑dimensional parameter manifold \text{Diff}(S^1)/\text{SL}(2,\mathbb{R}) and compute the QFIM. The pullback of this metric under the standard exponential map yields the exact Euclidean AdS₂ metric. This resolves the open problem identified in earlier exploratory attempts and provides the first solid proof‑of‑principle for the QFIM pullback mechanism.

Second, we prove a Holographic Fisher Bound: for any bulk spatial region V with boundary area A(\partial V), the integrated normal Fisher information density satisfies \int_V \sqrt{h}\, n^\mu n^\nu F_{\mu\nu} \le A(\partial V)/(4 L_P^2). The proof combines the quantum null energy condition with the Ryu–Takayanagi entropy formula. As a direct corollary, all polynomial curvature invariants are bounded by C L_P^{-4}, resolving classical singularities into finite‑curvature cores. This establishes, for the first time, a purely information‑theoretic mechanism for singularity resolution.

Third, we turn to cosmology and demonstrate that the information‑geometric sector of the action, containing an entropic potential \Lambda(S), can exactly reproduce the background expansion history of the \LambdaCDM model. We provide an explicit existence proof: given any smooth expansion law H(a), the functions \Lambda(S) and the kinetic term can be reconstructed so that the modified Einstein equations are satisfied. This elevates the earlier phenomenological sketches to a mathematically rigorous dark‑sector model.

All derivations are analytic and self‑contained. The framework opens a path from quantum information to observable gravitational physics without speculative assumptions, and it identifies clear open problems for future numerical and experimental investigation.

