Chapter 1: Introduction

1.1 The Quantum Gravity Problem and the Information‑Theoretic Turn

The unification of general relativity and quantum mechanics remains the central open problem in fundamental physics. Over the past two decades, a growing body of evidence has indicated that spacetime geometry is not fundamental but rather an emergent phenomenon rooted in the structure of quantum entanglement and information. This thesis takes that perspective seriously and constructs a concrete mathematical framework in which the spacetime metric is derived from a well‑defined information‑theoretic object: the Quantum Fisher Information Metric (QFIM).

The thesis rests on three well‑established pillars that have individually reshaped our understanding of quantum gravity:

1. Holographic duality [Maldacena 1999]: The AdS/CFT correspondence demonstrates that a gravitational bulk with one extra dimension can be encoded entirely in a non‑gravitational boundary quantum field theory. The bulk spacetime, its causal structure, and even its equations of motion are emergent properties of the boundary entanglement.
2. Entanglement thermodynamics [Jacobson 1995, 2016]: Einstein’s equations can be derived from the proportionality of entropy to horizon area for local Rindler horizons. Gravity appears as an entropic force, with the Einstein tensor arising from the equilibrium condition \delta S = \delta E/T.
3. Fisher information in gravitational actions [Matsueda 2013; Takeuchi 2018, 2020; recent preprints 2025–2026]: The Einstein–Hilbert action can be rewritten in terms of the Fisher information metric on a statistical manifold of states, revealing a deep structural analogy between information geometry and gravity.

Despite the profound insights offered by these works, a complete, mathematically rigorous mechanism that derives the full spacetime metric—with its dynamics, curvature, and cosmological behaviour—purely from quantum information quantities has remained elusive. The core hypothesis of this thesis closes that gap.

1.2 Core Hypothesis

The spacetime metric g_{\mu\nu} emerges as the pullback of the Quantum Fisher Information Metric F_{ab} of a suitably chosen family of quantum states. Distances in spacetime directly measure the statistical distinguishability of these states, and spacetime curvature reflects gradients of quantum correlations. The full gravitational action contains the standard Einstein–Hilbert term plus an information‑geometric sector with an entropic potential \Lambda(S) that depends on the local entanglement entropy.

Formally, if \mathcal{M} is the D-dimensional spacetime and \Lambda is the parameter manifold with coordinates \lambda^a labelling the states, then

g_{\mu\nu}(x) = \frac{\partial \lambda^a}{\partial x^\mu} \frac{\partial \lambda^b}{\partial x^\nu} F_{ab}(\lambda(x)).

This hypothesis is both economical and powerful. It requires no extra physical ingredients—no strings, loops, or extra dimensions—beyond the standard tools of quantum information and differential geometry. It makes spacetime geometry a secondary, derived concept whose properties are entirely determined by the information geometry of the underlying quantum system.

1.3 Scope and Methodology

The research is purely theoretical. No new experiments, data sets, or numerical codes are produced. All derivations rely on published analytical results from the high‑energy physics and quantum information literature, primarily from the arXiv preprint server, and are verified symbolically where needed. The aim is to provide a rigorous conceptual framework, identify its mathematical consequences, and isolate the open problems that must be addressed before the framework can become a fully predictive theory.

1.4 Contributions of this Dissertation

While the pullback idea and information‑geometric actions have appeared in various forms in recent literature, this thesis makes three specific, novel contributions that are presented with complete proofs or existence theorems:

· A rigorous derivation of JT gravity from the SYK QFIM (Chapter 4). Earlier attempts had restricted the parameter manifold to temperature and time and failed to reproduce the correct AdS₂ metric. We show that the proper parameter manifold is the coset space \text{Diff}(S^1)/\text{SL}(2,\mathbb{R}) of boundary time reparametrisations. Computing the QFIM on this manifold and performing the pullback yields the exact Euclidean AdS₂ metric, with the AdS radius expressed in terms of the SYK parameters. This is the first fully successful mapping of a known holographic duality into the QFIM pullback scheme.
· The Holographic Fisher Bound and singularity resolution (Chapter 5). We formulate and prove a bound relating the integrated normal Fisher information density to the area of the bounding surface: \int_V \sqrt{h}\, \mathcal{F} \le A(\partial V)/(4L_P^2). The proof relies on the quantum null energy condition and the Ryu–Takayanagi formula. As a corollary, all curvature invariants are shown to be bounded at the Planck scale, providing a first‑principles resolution of classical singularities without any ad‑hoc quantum corrections.
· Cosmological existence theorem (Chapter 6). We demonstrate that the entropic potential \Lambda(S) in the information action can be chosen such that the modified Friedmann equations exactly reproduce the background expansion history of the \LambdaCDM model. We provide an explicit reconstruction algorithm and prove that smooth solutions exist for any given H(a). This transforms the earlier sketches into a rigorous dark‑sector model, with the information fluid acting as both dark matter and dark energy in appropriate limits.

