Chapter 2: Literature Review and Novelty Assessment

2.1 Fisher Information in Gravity and Holography

The idea that information-theoretic quantities lie at the heart of gravitational physics has a history spanning over a decade. The earliest explicit connection between Fisher information and the Einstein equations was made by Matsueda (2013), who showed that the Einstein tensor can be derived as the Fisher information metric on a statistical manifold of states associated with a spatial foliation. In this picture, the metric of space is literally a measure of the distinguishability of nearby quantum states, and the Einstein field equations emerge as a natural geometric condition on that statistical manifold.

Takeuchi (2018, 2020) extended this line of thought by demonstrating that the two‑dimensional Einstein–Hilbert action can be rewritten entirely as the trace of a Fisher information metric. In the 2018 paper, Takeuchi performed a coarse‑graining analysis, showing that the renormalisation group flow of the Fisher metric reproduces the Ricci flow of the emergent geometry. In the 2020 work, the same author showed that the full Einstein–Hilbert action in two dimensions—including the cosmological constant term—can be expressed as the integral of the Fisher scalar curvature, thereby establishing a precise dictionary between information geometry and gravitational dynamics.

Parallel to these developments, Jacobson (1995) derived the Einstein equations from the proportionality of entropy to area for local Rindler horizons. In this thermodynamic perspective, the Einstein tensor is an equation of state, and the gravitational dynamics arise from the Clausius relation \delta Q = T dS applied to a local horizon. Jacobson’s 2016 paper extended the argument to include higher‑curvature corrections, further cementing the idea that gravity is an emergent, entropic phenomenon. Although Jacobson’s derivation does not explicitly mention Fisher information, the connection is immediate: the entropy variation dS along a null surface can be expressed in terms of the relative entropy between the vacuum and the excited state, and the second variation of the relative entropy is precisely the Fisher information.

More recently, a 2026 preprint titled “Information Geometry and the Master Action of the Directional Vacuum” introduced an action functional that couples the Einstein–Hilbert term to Fisher transport terms derived from a directional vacuum state. This work produces modified Einstein equations with an informational source and provides a first hint that the spacetime metric might be obtained as a pullback of the QFIM.

At the same time, several Zenodo preprints (2025–2026) have put forward ambitious proposals in which spacetime emerges from quantum information geometry. One prominent example combines the QFIM pullback with structures from Loop Quantum Gravity, such as the Immirzi parameter and spin networks, and makes specific predictions for dark matter. While these proposals are conceptually close to our own, they introduce additional physical ingredients that go beyond the purely information‑theoretic setting we wish to explore.

