2.4 Additional Context

Beyond the works directly centred on Fisher information, the past decade has witnessed an explosion of ideas connecting quantum information and spacetime emergence. Holographic complexity proposals (Susskind, Brown et al.) associate the growth of the wormhole behind the horizon with the quantum computational complexity of the boundary state. Quantum error correction and tensor network constructions (Swingle, Pastawski et al.) model the emergent bulk as an isometric encoding of the boundary Hilbert space, where the Ryu–Takayanagi surface is interpreted as a cut in the network. These developments are highly complementary to our framework. In particular, the QFIM can be understood as the metric on the space of tensor network states under infinitesimal variations, and our pullback construction may provide a continuous analogue of the discrete tensor network geometries.

Our work does not aim to replace these approaches but to supplement them with a concrete, analytically tractable model that connects directly to a solvable holographic system. The results of this thesis demonstrate that the QFIM pullback is not merely a formal analogy but a viable mathematical mechanism capable of reproducing established gravitational phenomena and generating new, testable predictions.

