Appendix A. Properties of the Quantum Fisher Information Metric

A.1 Pure‑State QFIM and the Fubini–Study Metric

Let |\psi(\lambda)\rangle be a smooth family of normalized vectors in a Hilbert space \mathcal{H}. The Fubini–Study metric on the projective space \mathbb{P}(\mathcal{H}) is the Kähler metric whose real part is

ds_{\rm FS}^2 = \langle d\psi | d\psi \rangle - |\langle \psi | d\psi \rangle|^2 .

The QFIM is defined as four times the real part of the Fubini–Study metric:

F_{ab} = 4\,\mathrm{Re}\big( \langle \partial_a\psi | \partial_b\psi \rangle - \langle \partial_a\psi | \psi\rangle\langle\psi | \partial_b\psi \rangle \big). \tag{A.1}

Proof of equivalence to projector form. With P = |\psi\rangle\langle\psi|, we have \langle\partial_a\psi | \psi\rangle\langle\psi | \partial_b\psi\rangle = \langle\partial_a\psi | P | \partial_b\psi\rangle. Hence

\langle \partial_a\psi | \partial_b\psi \rangle - \langle\partial_a\psi | \psi\rangle\langle\psi | \partial_b\psi \rangle
= \langle \partial_a\psi | (1-P) | \partial_b\psi \rangle .

Since 1-P is an orthogonal projector, the real part is non‑negative. This proves F_{ab} \ge 0 as a quadratic form.

Reparameterisation covariance. Under \lambda \mapsto \tilde\lambda, chain rule gives \partial_a = (\partial\tilde\lambda^c/\partial\lambda^a) \tilde\partial_c. Substituting into (A.1) yields the tensor transformation law \tilde F_{ab} = (\partial\lambda^c/\partial\tilde\lambda^a)(\partial\lambda^d/\partial\tilde\lambda^b) F_{cd}.

A.2 Mixed‑State QFIM and the Symmetric Logarithmic Derivative

For a family of density matrices \rho(\lambda), the symmetric logarithmic derivative (SLD) operators L_a are defined by

\partial_a \rho = \tfrac12 (\rho L_a + L_a \rho). \tag{A.2}

The SLD is unique on the support of \rho and can be chosen to satisfy \operatorname{Tr}(\rho L_a) = 0. The QFIM is

F_{ab} = \tfrac12 \operatorname{Tr}\!\big[ \rho \{L_a, L_b\} \big]
= \sum_{k,l} \frac{2\,\mathrm{Re}\big( \langle k|\partial_a\rho | l\rangle \langle l|\partial_b\rho | k\rangle \big)}{p_k + p_l}, \tag{A.3}

where p_k are the eigenvalues and \{|k\rangle\} the eigenvectors of \rho. The second form is manifestly basis‑independent and reduces to the pure‑state formula when \rho is rank‑one.

Proof of equivalence. Write \rho = \sum_k p_k |k\rangle\langle k|. Differentiating and comparing with (A.2) one finds \langle k|L_a | l\rangle = 2\langle k|\partial_a\rho | l\rangle/(p_k+p_l) for p_k+p_l>0. Substituting into the trace reproduces the sum over k,l. ∎

Positive semi‑definiteness. For any real vector v^a,

v^a v^b F_{ab} = \tfrac12 \operatorname{Tr}\big[ \rho (v^a L_a)^2 \big] + \tfrac12 \operatorname{Tr}\big[ \rho (v^a L_a)^2 \big] \ge 0,

since \rho is positive and (v^a L_a)^2 is positive semi‑definite.

Bures metric. The Bures distance between \rho and \rho+d\rho is D_B^2 = \frac12 \operatorname{Tr}[d\rho \, \mathcal{L}_\rho^{-1}(d\rho)]. Because \mathcal{L}_\rho^{-1}(d\rho) = \frac12 (\rho \, dL + dL \, \rho) with dL the SLD, the Bures metric coincides with the QFIM when evaluated on the tangent vectors \partial_a\rho.

A.3 Fisher–Entropy Relations

For an exponential family \rho(\beta) = e^{-\beta H}/Z(\beta), the SLD is L_\beta = - (H - \langle H\rangle). Hence

F_{\beta\beta} = \operatorname{Var}(H) = \partial_\beta^2 \ln Z.

From thermodynamic identities \partial S/\partial \beta = -\beta \operatorname{Var}(H) one deduces

F_{\beta\beta} = \frac{1}{\beta^2} \frac{(\partial S/\partial\beta)^2}{F_{\beta\beta}} \;\Longrightarrow\; F_{\beta\beta} \ge \frac{1}{\beta} \left|\frac{\partial S}{\partial\beta}\right|.

This is the quantum Cramér–Rao bound for the parameter \beta with the entropy as estimator.

