Appendix B. SYK Model and the Schwarzian QFIM: Detailed Derivation

B.1 Large‑N Action and Conformal Solution

Starting from the disordered SYK Hamiltonian, the disorder‑averaged partition function at large N is governed by the bilocal action

I[G,\Sigma] = -\frac{N}{2} \ln\det(\delta(\tau_1-\tau_2)\partial_\tau - \Sigma)
+ \frac{N}{2} \int d\tau_1 d\tau_2 \left( \Sigma G - \frac{J^2}{q} G^q \right). \tag{B.1}

The saddle‑point equations \delta I/\delta G = 0 = \delta I/\delta \Sigma yield

G(i\omega_n)^{-1} = i\omega_n - \Sigma(i\omega_n), \qquad \Sigma(\tau) = J^2 G(\tau)^{q-1}.

At low energies the kinetic term i\omega_n is negligible compared to the self‑energy. The equation becomes G \star \Sigma = 1 (convolution), which is invariant under any reparametrisation of time \tau \to f(\tau). The conformal solution is

G_c(\tau) = b \,\mathrm{sgn}(\tau)\, |J\tau|^{-2\Delta}, \qquad \Delta = 1/q,

with b a constant.

B.2 Effective Action for the Soft Mode

The soft mode is the reparametrisation f(\tau). Fluctuations around the conformal saddle are captured by writing G(\tau_1,\tau_2) = G_f(\tau_1,\tau_2) and integrating out the massive fluctuations. One obtains the Schwarzian effective action

S_{\mathrm{Sch}}[f] = -\frac{N\alpha_S}{J} \int_0^\beta d\tau \, \{f,\tau\},
\qquad \{f,\tau\} = \frac{f'''}{f'} - \frac32 \left(\frac{f''}{f'}\right)^2. \tag{B.2}

The dimensionless coefficient \alpha_S depends on q and is positive. The action is \text{SL}(2,\mathbb{R})-invariant.

B.3 Quadratic Expansion and Propagator

Expanding around a basepoint f_0(\tau) = \tau + \epsilon(\tau), the quadratic part reads

S^{(2)}[\epsilon] = \frac{N\alpha_S}{J} \int_0^\beta d\tau \left[ \frac12 (\epsilon'')^2 - \frac32 (\epsilon')^2 \right].

Fourier expanding on the circle, \epsilon(\tau) = \sum_{n} \epsilon_n e^{2\pi i n \tau/\beta}, one finds

S^{(2)} = \frac{2\pi^2 N\alpha_S}{J\beta^3} \sum_n n^2(n^2-1) |\epsilon_n|^2.

The gauge modes n=-1,0,1 correspond to \text{SL}(2,\mathbb{R}); they have vanishing action and must be omitted from the path integral. The propagator is

\langle \epsilon_n \epsilon_{-m} \rangle = \frac{J\beta^3}{4\pi^2 N\alpha_S} \frac{\delta_{n,m}}{n^2(n^2-1)}, \qquad |n|\ge 2.

B.4 QFIM from the Boundary Overlap

The family of TFD states is |\psi(f)\rangle = U(f)|\text{TFD}\rangle. The QFIM component along tangent vectors \delta f_1, \delta f_2 is

F(\delta f_1,\delta f_2) = 4\,\mathrm{Re}\langle \text{TFD}| U^{-1}\partial_1 U \cdot U^{-1}\partial_2 U |\text{TFD}\rangle_c .

Using the path integral representation, the connected correlator equals the inverse of the quadratic action (up to normalization). Hence, for the Fourier basis, the QFIM is diagonal:

F_{n,m} = 4 \times \frac{4\pi^2 N\alpha_S}{J\beta^3} n^2(n^2-1) \,\delta_{n+m,0}
= \frac{16\pi^2 N\alpha_S}{J\beta^3} n^2(n^2-1) \,\delta_{n+m,0}. \tag{B.3}

B.5 Two‑Dimensional Truncation and Pullback to AdS₂

To obtain a 2D metric, we select the submanifold generated by time translations and scale transformations. The time translation is the \tau direction, generated by H (the n=0 Fourier component, which is not in the Schwarzian sector but corresponds to the Hamiltonian). The scale transformation is the radial direction, encoded by the n=\pm 2 mode if we think of a finite cutoff. More directly, using the dictionary between the UV cutoff \epsilon and the radial coordinate \sigma = -\ln \epsilon, the QFIM components along (t,\sigma) are

F_{tt} = 4\operatorname{Var}(H) = \frac{8\pi^2 N\alpha_S}{J\beta^3},
\qquad
F_{\sigma\sigma} = \frac{8\pi^2 N\alpha_S}{J\beta^3} e^{2\sigma},
\qquad
F_{t\sigma} = 0.

The latter follows from the conformal invariance: the variance of the scale generator equals that of H up to the Jacobian d\sigma = -d\epsilon/\epsilon. In the near‑boundary limit \sigma \to -\infty (or \epsilon \to 0), this reproduces the Poincaré metric

ds^2 = F_{tt} dt^2 + F_{\sigma\sigma} d\sigma^2
= A e^{2\sigma}(dt^2 + d\sigma^2),
\qquad A = \frac{8\pi^2 N\alpha_S}{J\beta^3}.

Defining z = A^{-1/2} e^{-\sigma} yields

ds^2 = \frac{dt^2 + dz^2}{z^2},

the Euclidean AdS₂ metric with unit radius. The physical AdS radius is L^2 = A.

